  "id": "pixels-numbers",
  "after": "overview",
  "title": "To a computer, a photo is a grid of numbers.",
  "caption": "A tiny 8 × 8 photo of a tomato on a yellow cloth. Each colour pixel is three numbers from 0 to 255: how much red, green and blue it has. A grey photo needs only one number per pixel. A normal phone photo is crores of numbers, and a CNN reads only these numbers.",
  "alt": "Left: an 8 by 8 grid of coloured squares that forms a red tomato with a green leaf on a yellow cloth. Three squares are outlined and linked to labels: a leaf pixel is red 60, green 150, blue 50; a yellow cloth pixel is red 230, green 170, blue 20; a tomato pixel is red 210, green 40, blue 30. Below: each colour pixel is 3 numbers from 0 to 255, so 8 by 8 pixels make 192 numbers; a grey pixel is 1 number, 0 for black and 255 for white; a 4,000 by 3,000 phone photo is 3.6 crore numbers.",
