  "id": "growing-view",
  "after": "overview",
  "title": "Each layer sees a bigger patch of the photo.",
  "caption": "A small CNN looking at a 32 × 32 photo of a tomato. One neuron (one small unit of a layer) in layer 1 sees only 3 × 3 pixels, enough for a bit of edge. Pooling halves the map between layers, so a neuron in layer 3 already covers 18 × 18 pixels: the stem and the round skin. Each map is labelled height × width × number of filters. The maps shrink while the number of filters grows, until the network can answer \"ripe\".",
  "alt": "A tomato photo with three nested squares of 3, 8 and 18 pixels, showing how much of the image one neuron in layers 1, 2 and 3 can see: tiny edges and colours, then curves and textures, then parts like the stem and round skin. Below, a row of feature maps shrinks from 32 by 32 by 16 to 16 by 16 by 32 to 8 by 8 by 64, with a pool step between each, before the answer ripe.",
